course: dictionary
lesson: 8934
title: Signposts in the road and in the text
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A signpost is a post by the side of a road or path, with signs that show the way and how far it is to places. Its job is to guide you, so you know where to go next.
explain: The word also works in writing. «A comma is a tiny signpost that tells the reader where one part of a sentence ends and the next begins.» The comma does not say anything itself, but it shows the reader where to go.
explain: Words such as firstly, next and finally are signposts too. They show the reader what is coming. When you read a text, look for them: they are the road signs of the writing, and they help you find your way through the ideas.
fact: A signpost is a post at the side of a road or path with signs that show the way and how far it is to places.
fact: «A signpost arrow shows the way to a town and the number shows how far it is.» In the UK that number is in miles.
fact: Signpost can also mean something that shows what is coming, like headings in a text.
fact: To signpost something means to mark the way to it with signs.
fact: Signpost words such as «firstly», «next» and «finally» guide the reader through a text.
exercise: «A signpost arrow shows the way to a town and the number shows how far it is.» What does the number on a signpost show? | The name of the town | How many people live there | *How far it is to the town | The price of a map
exercise: In a non-fiction text, which word is a signpost word? | *Finally | Quickly | Hedgehog | Blue
exercise: To signpost something means... | To lose your way on a hike | Wash it clean | *To mark the way to it with signs | To read it aloud
test: «In non-fiction, headings, subheadings and signposts such as "Firstly, secondly, finally" organise the text.» What does organise mean here? | Delete the headings | *Put into a clear order so the reader can follow | Colour in the pictures | Make the text shorter
test: Jess says the headings "Habitat" and "Diet" are just decoration. Why are they signposts? | They make the page heavier | They are the names of the authors | They stop the reader from reading | *They show what is coming in the text, so the reader can find the right part
exam: «A comma is a tiny signpost that tells the reader where one part of a sentence ends and the next begins.» Which job does the comma do? | Shows the reader where one part of a sentence ends | Replaces the full stop at the end of every sentence | *Shows the reader where one part ends and the next begins | Makes the sentence longer
